          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal words directly avow Goneril’s loving emotional state toward Lear. The rest of her turn elaborates the asserted love through comparisons and hyperbole, making truth-presenting avowal the expression’s clearest mode, regardless of sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"The occurrence does not itself undertake a future action, renew an obligation, or position Goneril as bound to specified filial duties. Her references to a child’s love characterize the magnitude of the state she claims rather than affirming an obligation. Lear’s subsequent territorial grant is a separate act rewarding the declaration, not uptake of a promise made by these words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Of all these bounds, even from this line to this,\\nWith shadowy forests and with champains rich’d,\\nWith plenteous rivers and wide-skirted meads,\\nWe make thee lady:\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs in an extended, rhetorically ordered answer to Lear’s formal request. Nothing represents it as a reflex-like affective outburst or expressive discharge beyond the avowal itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\\n\\nGONERIL.\\nSir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression has a substantial persuasive and self-presentational function: it answers a competitive solicitation in which Lear explicitly links professed love to the size of each daughter’s bounty. The focal avowal therefore helps advance Goneril’s claim to filial merit and reward, a function not adequately captured by T, P, or E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a formal court proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to declare which loves him most and explicitly connects their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Goneril, speaking first, gives an elaborate and competitive profession of limitless filial love. Lear then grants her territory.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The supplied evidence establishes no distinct undertaking by Goneril in the focal expression: she claims an extreme degree of filial love but does not promise conduct or affirm herself as bound to duties. Lear treats the declaration as satisfying his love test and rewards it with territory, but that is uptake of its avowal and persuasive claim, not acceptance of a promise. The avowal supplies positive verbal evidence of affection, while later supplied passages describing the sisters’ harms and wrongdoing provide contrary evidence; they do not retroactively erase the original utterance’s avowal force or conclusively settle Goneril’s inner state at that moment.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"Recognised as William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The classification is based only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly establishes the utterance’s public avowal and strategic setting but cannot conclusively determine Goneril’s actual feelings at the moment of speaking. Later wrongdoing bears on interpretation and sincerity but does not by itself prove that no affection existed then.\"}"
        }
